WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.875
To sum up.

2
00:00:00.875 --> 00:00:05.439
You built your understanding of a check
from its catalog entry to its safety tests,

3
00:00:05.439 --> 00:00:08.190
and saw how one check asks
for the least privilege

4
00:00:08.190 --> 00:00:10.875
it can when its read needs
the most powerful role.

5
00:00:10.875 --> 00:00:14.524
The evidence an ISSO keeps:
the run's results, details,

6
00:00:14.524 --> 00:00:18.428
summary and validation
manifest, in the evidence store;

7
00:00:18.428 --> 00:00:24.114
the window date and its change record; the
Entra sign-in log for the one privileged

8
00:00:24.114 --> 00:00:29.800
export; and the change record for any
remediation, which is never made by the check.

9
00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:34.225
Before shipping a new check, work
through the Chapter 03 checklist.

10
00:00:34.225 --> 00:00:37.975
Next is Module 3: keep
dynamic rules as code.

11
00:00:37.975 --> 00:00:43.625
A dynamic rule is access policy, and one
edit can remove hundreds of people at once.

12
00:00:43.625 --> 00:00:47.674
You'll keep each rule as a reviewed
file with tests and a linter,

13
00:00:47.674 --> 00:00:52.730
see whom a change adds and removes, and
pause and resume safely in an incident.
